HARMONIA-DSL

HARMONIA-DSL is an experimental domain-specific language (DSL) and runtime for exploring constraint-driven, agent-assisted computation and recursive system behavior.

It combines:

a custom syntax layer

an interpreter / execution runtime

optional AI agent integration

structured artifacts for experimenting with non-collapsing system design

This project is part of a broader effort to study how explicit constraints, symbolic structure, and agent mediation can be used to build more robust computational systems.

🔍 What This Project Is (and Is Not)
✅ What it is

A working DSL prototype with:

syntax definitions

a parser / interpreter

runtime execution paths

A sandbox for systems thinking, not just language syntax

A place to test constraint semantics, state transitions, and agent-assisted execution

A bridge between symbolic structure and practical tooling

❌ What it is not

Not a finished language specification

Not a production framework (yet)

Not a typical “prompt language”

Not tied to a single AI model or vendor

🧠 Core Concepts

HARMONIA-DSL explores a few core ideas:

Constraint-first design
Constraints are treated as primary, not as after-the-fact validation.

Explicit structure over implicit behavior
Execution paths are meant to be legible and inspectable.

Agent-assisted interpretation
Optional AI agents can participate in interpretation, evaluation, or augmentation — but are not required.

Non-collapse under recursion
The language and runtime are designed to tolerate iterative and recursive use without undefined or runaway states.

🛠 Repository Structure (High-Level)

Typical components you’ll find here:

Parser / Interpreter

Core logic for reading and executing HARMONIA-DSL syntax

Runtime / Launchers

Scripts for running the DSL standalone or with AI agents

Agent Integrations

Optional hooks for local LLMs (e.g. Ollama-based agents)

Language Artifacts

.hrm files representing example programs, configurations, or experiments

Specs & Definitions

Syntax notes, design documents, and evolving specifications

Tests

Basic validation of interpreter behavior

The repo intentionally contains both code and artifacts, reflecting its role as a research-driven systems workspace.

🚀 Minimal Usage (Example)

Exact commands may evolve — this shows the intended flow.

# run a basic HARMONIA program
python launch_harm.py examples/example.hrm


Or, with agent assistance enabled:

python launch_harm_ai.py examples/example.hrm


Outputs typically include:

execution traces

structured logs

constraint evaluations

optional agent commentary

🎯 Why This Exists

Most modern software systems:

optimize early

hide constraints

rely on implicit behavior

collapse under recursive or adversarial use

HARMONIA-DSL exists to explore the opposite approach:

Make structure explicit, make constraints first-class, and treat stability as a design goal.

📌 Status

Experimental

Actively evolving

Intended for:

systems exploration

language design experiments

agent orchestration research

personal and collaborative R&D

Expect rough edges — that’s the point.

🧩 Relationship to Other Work

HARMONIA-DSL is related to:

hybrid symbolic / subsymbolic systems

DSL and language runtime design

agent-based computation

system integrity and failure-mode analysis

It is also a sibling project to RI1-HYBRID-ENGINE, where similar ideas are explored at a broader platform level.

🤝 Contributions & Discussion

This project is currently driven by a single author, but:

feedback is welcome

